1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,560
Section 4

2
00:00:01,560 --> 00:00:08,516
As you all know, the university is planning an arts festival for later this

3
00:00:08,516 --> 00:00:08,897
year,

4
00:00:08,897 --> 00:00:14,390
and here in the music department we've planned three concerts.

5
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:18,352
These will be public performances,

6
00:00:18,352 --> 00:00:21,650
and the programme has just been finalised.

7
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:28,263
The theme of the festival is links between the UK and Australia,

8
00:00:28,263 --> 00:00:30,688
and this is reflected in the music:

9
00:00:30,688 --> 00:00:36,400
each concert will feature both British and Australian composers.

10
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:43,396
I'll tell you briefly about the Australian music, as you probably won't be familiar

11
00:00:43,396 --> 00:00:44,180
with that.

12
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:49,900
The first concert will include music by Liza Lim,

13
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:55,460
who was born in Perth, Western Australia, in 1966.

14
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:02,474
As a child, Lim originally learned to play the piano-like so many children

15
00:01:02,474 --> 00:01:04,698
and also the violin,

16
00:01:04,698 --> 00:01:10,910
but when she was 11 her teachers encouraged her to start composing.

17
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:14,724
She found this was her real strength,

18
00:01:14,724 --> 00:01:22,170
and she studied and later taught composition, both in Australia and in other countries.

19
00:01:22,170 --> 00:01:30,218
As a composer, she has received commissions from numerous orchestras, other performers

20
00:01:30,218 --> 00:01:33,770
and festivals in several countries.

21
00:01:33,770 --> 00:01:39,622
Liza Lim's compositions are vibrant and full of energy,

22
00:01:39,622 --> 00:01:48,139
and she often explores Asian and Australian Aboriginal cultural sources, including the native instrument,

23
00:01:48,139 --> 00:01:49,241
the didgeridoo:

24
00:01:49,241 --> 00:01:53,550
this is featured in a work called The Compass.

25
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:57,960
Her music is very expressive,

26
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:04,215
so although it is complex, it has the power of connecting with audiences

27
00:02:04,215 --> 00:02:06,400
and performers alike.

28
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:13,533
In the festival we're going to give a semi-staged performance of The Oresteia.

29
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:21,403
This is an opera in seven parts, based on the trilogy of ancient Greek

30
00:02:21,403 --> 00:02:23,070
tragedies by Aeschylus.

31
00:02:23,070 --> 00:02:27,944
Lim composed this when she was in her mid-20s,

32
00:02:27,944 --> 00:02:33,360
and she also wrote the text, along with Barrie Kosky.

33
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:37,503
lt's performed by six singers, a dancer,

34
00:02:37,503 --> 00:02:45,198
and an orchestra that, as well as standard orchestral instruments, includes electric guitar,

35
00:02:45,198 --> 00:02:48,750
and a traditional Turkish stringed instrument.

36
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:56,595
Lim wrote that because the stories in the tragedies are not easy to tell,

37
00:02:56,595 --> 00:03:00,517
the sounds she creates are also disturbing,

38
00:03:00,517 --> 00:03:05,000
and they include breathing, sobbing, laughing and whistling.

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,200
The work lasts around 75 minutes,

40
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:17,800
and the rest of the concert will consist of orchestral works by the British composers Ralph Vaughan Williams

41
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,400
and Frederick Delius.

42
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:30,195
Moving on now to our second concert, this will begin with instrumental music by British composers Benjamin Britten

43
00:03:30,195 --> 00:03:31,994
and Judith Weir.

44
00:03:31,994 --> 00:03:39,791
After the interval we'll go to Australia for a piece by Ross Edwards:

45
00:03:39,791 --> 00:03:42,190
The Tower of Remoteness.

46
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:47,403
According to Edwards, the inspiration for this piece came from nature,

47
00:03:47,403 --> 00:03:54,037
when he was sitting alone in the dry bed of a creek, overshadowed by

48
00:03:54,037 --> 00:03:59,250
the leaves of palm trees, listening to the birds and insects.

49
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:04,998
The Tower of Remoteness is scored for piano and clarinet.

50
00:04:04,998 --> 00:04:13,046
Edwards says he realised years after writing the piece that he had subconsciously modelled

51
00:04:13,046 --> 00:04:17,070
its opening phrase on a bird call.

52
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:22,609
Ross Edwards was born in 1943 in Sydney, Australia,

53
00:04:22,609 --> 00:04:30,609
and studied at the Sydney Conservatorium of Music and the universities of Adelaide

54
00:04:30,609 --> 00:04:31,840
and Sydney.

55
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,540
He's well known in Australia,

56
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:40,480
and in fact he's one of the country's most performed composers.

57
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:49,605
He's written a wide range of music, from symphonies and concertos to some composed

58
00:04:49,605 --> 00:04:51,560
specifically for children.

59
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:57,397
Edwards's music has been described as being 'deeply connected to Australia',

60
00:04:57,397 --> 00:05:04,827
and it can be regarded as a celebration of the diversity of cultures that

61
00:05:04,827 --> 00:05:07,480
Australia can be proud of.

62
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:15,905
The last of the three Australian composers to be represented in our festival is

63
00:05:15,905 --> 00:05:17,109
Carl Vine.

64
00:05:17,109 --> 00:05:24,330
Born in 1954, Vine, like Liza Lim, comes from Perth, Western Australia.

65
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:31,620
He took up the cornet at the age of five, switching to the piano

66
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:33,182
five years later.

67
00:05:33,182 --> 00:05:39,430
However, he went to university to study physics, before changing to composition.

68
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:46,631
After graduating he moved to Sydney and worked as a freelance pianist and composer.

69
00:05:46,631 --> 00:05:53,317
Before long he had become prominent in Australia as a composer for dance,

70
00:05:53,317 --> 00:05:58,460
and in fact has written 25 scores of that type.

71
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:06,319
In our third concert, Vine will be represented by his music for the flag

72
00:06:06,319 --> 00:06:10,810
hand-over ceremony of the Olympics held in 1996.

73
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:17,742
This seven-minute orchestral piece was of course heard by millions of people worldwide,

74
00:06:17,742 --> 00:06:24,141
and we'll hear it alongside works written by British composers Edward Elgar

75
00:06:24,141 --> 00:06:27,340
and, more recently, Thomas Adès.
